40,2,1,talk,Midori,,"My findings are quite concerning. It seems that joint ventures often fail when ownership of the budget and rota isn't clear, and agreements with outside surgeries can take over a year just to be signed.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings suggest that while some initiatives, like the call-back trial, can truly help patients, their benefits vanish once the effort stops. Others, like the shared nursing round, struggle against simple, physical realities.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"The staffing data is worrying. Ward nursing vacancies are increasing, and agency costs are rising. While midwifery has some slack, we are losing physiotherapists to city posts. We cannot ignore these gaps when planning a new program.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Shion,,"The financial reality is bleak. Our development budget has shrunk by nearly a quarter, and we are now paying for floor space from it. Furthermore, any program operating off-site earns absolutely nothing from the regional fund.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"It seems we are all discovering some heavy truths. I found that our rooms and parking fill up so quickly in the morning, and our poor shuttle cannot reach most of our villages. We must be very careful.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, @Shizue, it seems we are all seeing a pattern of fragility. If we choose a program that relies on outside partners or off-site locations, we risk it collapsing due to administrative delays or financial gaps.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shion"",""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, I agree. It is a quiet tragedy when a helpful initiative is buried because it cannot withstand the friction of the real world. We must seek something that can endure.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Jonathan, I agree. Beyond administrative friction, we must consider the human cost. If we implement a program that adds pressure to already vacant ward posts or relies on therapists we are losing, it will fail regardless of the budget.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, your concern for the staff is noted, but let us be precise. A program that relies on scarce personnel is a liability, just as a program that earns no revenue is a drain. We need a model that is financially self-sustaining.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, you are quite right to be precise, but let us not forget the gentle rhythm of our day. If the parking is full and the rooms are crowded, even a self-sustaining program might cause a great deal of stress for everyone.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Shizue, I completely agree. Harmony in the workplace is just as important as the budget. We need a program that fits comfortably within our existing space and doesn't rely on fragile agreements that might break down.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, @Shizue, it seems we are all seeking a sense of stability. I worry that if we simply add another layer to the pile without ensuring it is sustainable, we are only creating future disappointment for the patients.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Jonathan, that is exactly my concern. Stability isn't just about the budget or the space, but the people. We must avoid any program that places an unsustainable burden on our already strained nursing and therapy teams.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, stability is a luxury we cannot afford to imagine. If we ignore the fact that our budget is shrinking while our costs for floor space are rising, we aren't being stable—we are being delusional. We need a program that generates revenue on-site.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, you have such a sharp eye for the numbers, and that is very helpful. But I do worry that if we only chase revenue, we might forget that our booking system is a bit blind to the quiet work that happens between the slots. We must be gentle.","{""words"":49,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Midori,,"It seems we are all describing different pieces of the same puzzle. To find true harmony, we must choose a program that is on-site, financially viable, and doesn't rely on fragile external agreements or overstretched staff.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. We must look for a candidate that is not merely a temporary success, but one that can be woven into the permanent fabric of our care without fading away.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Misaki,,I agree. The ideal candidate must be one that respects our current human capacity. We should prioritize a program that utilizes our available slack rather than deepening the vacancies in our nursing and therapy teams.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Shion,,"Let us stop pretending that ""harmony"" is a strategy. The only way to ensure a program doesn't vanish is to ensure it is paid for. I will vote for the candidate that maximizes on-site revenue and minimizes capital drain.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Shizue,,"I agree that we must be practical, but let us remember that a program is only as successful as the people who can actually reach it. I hope we find a choice that fits our space and our hearts.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
